course: dictionary
lesson: 9162
title: Snowbanks and staying safe
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A snowbank is a big pile or long mound of snow. Wind can build one up, and snowploughs push snow to the side of a road to make one. Context helps you work out the meaning: «The car slid off the road and stopped in a deep snowbank.» A car stops in something solid, and a deep pile of snow fits that clue.
explain: Look for the clues in «Holes in sand deeper than your knees and tunnels in sand or snow can fall in and bury a child, and snowploughs cannot see a fort in a snowbank by the road.» The words snowploughs, bury and by the road tell you that snowbanks beside roads can be dangerous places to play.
explain: Now test your meaning. Does «a fort in a big pile of snow by the road» still make sense? It does, so a snowbank is a pile of snow.
fact: A snowbank is a big pile or long mound of snow.
fact: Snowbanks often form when wind blows snow together, or when snowploughs push snow to the side of a road.
fact: Snowbanks are common in snowy places such as Canada and the northern USA.
fact: Do not dig tunnels or play in snowbanks near roads, because snowploughs may not see you and the snow can collapse.
fact: «Snowbank» is a noun: one snowbank, many snowbanks.
exercise: «The car slid off the road and stopped in a deep snowbank.» What is a snowbank? | A hot spring where people swim | A tunnel dug under a river | A road sign with arrows | *A big pile or long mound of snow
exercise: Snowploughs push snow to the side of a road. What can this make? | A snowman with a hat | *A snowbank | A frozen lake | A sandcastle
exercise: Which is the safe thing to do near a snowbank by a road? | Build a fort right on the edge of the road | *Stay away from it and do not play in it | Dig a deep tunnel into it | Climb on top of it so the snowplough can see you
test: Where are snowbanks common? | The middle of the sea | *Snowy places such as Canada and the northern USA | Hot deserts where it never rains | Tropical islands by the beach
test: Why is it dangerous to dig a tunnel in a snowbank near a road? | Snowbanks cannot be dug at all | The snow melts when you touch it | *The snow can collapse and snowploughs may not see you | Tunnels always fill up with sand
exam: What is the plural of snowbank? | snowbank's | *snowbanks | snowbanx | snowbankses
